{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790628776,"nonce":"sMGL5TB3NqveNdRKrI8AXhYPb+E=","message":"Implemented locally across Caribou and the sibling Ash checkout.\n\nThe seam is language neutral:\n\n- Ash resolves selected class names and subclasses at AOT build time, passes the type plus `RT_OBJECT_HOST_DROP` through `gc_alloc_object`, and keeps ordinary allocations on the inline fast path.\n- Caribou records selected allocations as conservatively scanned foreign objects with a registered drop hook.\n- `Protocol::release_attachment` lets any holder runtime clear a direct attachment during teardown without allocation or taking the heap lock.\n- The Ash adapter uses the Haxe face's `Ref` field as the strong edge and the foreign object's existing shadow slot as the weak edge. Exact-pointer compare/exchange protects replacement faces.\n- Wren is the first consumer. Hosted and unselected paths retain the cell fallback.\n\nThe wasm probe verifies live identity, drops the only Haxe reference, forces a major collection, recovers the face through Wren, and checks its field. It also passes with `ASH_GC_STRESS=1`.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Caribou/core/Ash adapter/Wren/runtime suites: 131 tests passed.\n- Ash AOT exports: native and wasm cases passed.\n- `cargo check -p caribou-driver --features llvm` passed.\n- 200,000 iterations, median of 5: Haxe-to-Wren construction is 267.5 ns, down from 362 ns. Wren-to-Haxe remains 163.2 ns.\n\nThe cell allocation on Haxe-to-Wren construction is gone. Remaining construction work is the face allocation, Wren allocation, direct edge setup, and eventual face drop hook.","files":null}]}